@fredericBregier Thanks for explanation.
I use version 4.0. I use a ready-made 4.0.23 netty.

``` java
final public class DefaultChannelInitializer extends ChannelInitializer<SocketChannel> {
        @Override
        public void initChannel(SocketChannel channel) throws Exception {
            channel.pipeline().addLast(new ChannelTrafficShapingHandler());
            channel.pipeline().addLast(new ServerHandler(Server.this));
                        // other handlers
        }
    }
```

If I have installed a new speed limit in some other place, I call <code>AbstractTrafficShapingHandler.configure(long newWriteLimit, long newReadLimit)</code> 
 before call method write(). But in reality I set limit once and work with him.

In my example I set 5MB limit for write and read (5 \* 1024 \* 1024). At the same time, as I wrote earlier, shaper works with read() as expected, and when writing large amounts, it sends more than the set limit in several times.

@fredericBregier 
Your logs are very smooth (data goes smoothly in the method of <code>write()</code>. Are they linger somewhere?)
In my case, they quickly fall into method <code>write()</code>, where trafficCounter calculates delay time (he invokes <code>TrafficCounter.bytesWriteFlowControl(long write)</code>, and this value is displayed in the <code>toString ()</code>). So when <code>TrafficMonitoringTask</code> invokes <code>doAccounting </code>

``` java
    @Override
    protected void doAccounting(TrafficCounter counter) {
        Logger.getLogger(ChannelTrafficShapingHandler.class.getName()).info(counter.toString());
        super.doAccounting(counter);
    }
```

logger displays 0KB/s.
